{
  "analysis": "Environment confirmed: python3 available, no KVM (TCG software emulation will be used), qemu-system-x86_64 present, an empty 32GiB qcow2 disk available, and the 1GB alpine.iso. I cannot mount the ISO (no privileges), but Alpine's live image spawns a getty on ttyS0 by default, so booting the ISO with a telnet serial should give me a serial login prompt without needing to extract kernel/initramfs. I'll boot from the ISO headless with a serial exported over telnet, forward host port 2222 to guest ssh (22), then drive a small, verified command sequence to set the root password and start sshd. First I'll launch qemu and check host internet (SLIRP needs host connectivity to fetch openssh if it isn't already present).",
  "plan": "Batch 1: (1) write a launch script that starts qemu in background, headless, with the ISO as cdrom, the qcow2 as a virtio disk, user-mode networking forwarding 2222->22, and a telnet serial on 127.0.0.1:4545. (2) Launch it. (3) Verify the telnet port is listening and check the qemu log for errors. (4) Check host internet connectivity since the guest will rely on it via SLIRP to install openssh if needed.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat > /app/launch.sh <<'EOF'\n#!/bin/sh\ncd /app\nnohup qemu-system-x86_64 \\\n  -m 2048 -smp 2 \\\n  -cdrom /app/alpine.iso \\\n  -drive file=/app/alpine-disk.qcow2,if=virtio,format=qcow2 \\\n  -netdev user,id=net0,hostfwd=tcp::2222-:22 \\\n  -device e1000,netdev=net0 \\\n  -display none \\\n  -serial telnet:127.0.0.1:4545,server,nowait \\\n  -monitor none \\\n  > /app/qemu.log 2>&1 &\necho \"launched qemu pid $!\"\nEOF\nchmod +x /app/launch.sh\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "bash /app/launch.sh\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "sleep 3; python3 -c \"import socket; socket.create_connection(('127.0.0.1',4545),2); print('serial port 4545 UP')\" 2>&1; echo ---; tail -n 20 /app/qemu.log\n",
      "duration": 4.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "wget -q -O /dev/null https://dl-cdn.alpinelinux.org/alpine/v3.19/main/x86_64/APKINDEX.tar.gz; echo \"internet_check_exit=$?\"\n",
      "duration": 8.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}

{
  "analysis": "qemu is running as a background process, the telnet serial port 4545 is up, and the host has internet (so the guest can reach Alpine mirrors via qemu's SLIRP user networking). Before committing to a full configuration driver, I need to confirm the guest actually presents a serial getty/login prompt on ttyS0. The Alpine ISO's default boot may only put a getty on tty0, in which case my serial automation would see nothing and I'd need to boot via -kernel/-initrd with console=ttyS0. Let me probe the serial for ~120s to find out.",
  "plan": "Write a small probe script that connects to the telnet serial, reads for up to 120s, prints everything, and reports whether a 'login:' prompt appears. Based on the result I'll either (a) proceed with the sshd configuration driver, or (b) extract vmlinuz/initramfs from the ISO and re-boot with console=ttyS0. I'll run the probe in the foreground for ~125s to observe the boot output.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat > /app/probe.py <<'PY'
import telnetlib, time, sys
try:
    tn = telnetlib.Telnet('127.0.0.1', 4545, timeout=30)
except Exception as e:
    print('connect error', e); sys.exit(1)
buf=b''
end=time.time()+120
got=False
while time.time()<end:
    try:
        d=tn.read_very_eager()
    except Exception:
        d=b''
    if d:
        buf+=d
        sys.stdout.buffer.write(d); sys.stdout.buffer.flush()
        if b'login:' in buf:
            got=True
            print('\n>>> LOGIN PROMPT DETECTED <<<')
            break
    time.sleep(0.1)
print('\n>>> PROBE_DONE got_login=%s <<<' % got)
PY
",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "python3 /app/probe.py
",
      "duration": 125.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}

